<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Gravelotte</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Gravelotte–St. Privat · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Gravelotte, also called Gravelotte–St. Privat, was fought on 18 August 1870 west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> and was the largest battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>. German armies under Helmuth von Moltke, including a Hessian division, suffered heavy losses but forced Marshal Bazaine's French Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> back into Metz, where it was besieged and surrendered in October.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-gravelotte.jpg" alt="Battle of Gravelotte" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alphonse de Neuville · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlphonseNeuvilleFriedhofSaint-PrivatL1100806_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>On 16 August 1870 the German Second Army under Prince Friedrich Karl met the right wing of Marshal François Achille Bazaine's French Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> at the Battle of Mars-la-Tour, and both sides claimed victory. Bazaine's four corps retreated in bad weather along the road towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>, with the Germans pressing hard to stop them linking up with French forces at Sedan. Their pressure forced Bazaine to take the crests east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>, with his left flank at Rozerieulles and his right at St. Privat. Bazaine regarded the position as virtually impregnable, with the defenders sheltered behind hedges and low walls and anchored in villages and farmhouses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a>'s combined forces, the Prussian First and Second Armies, numbered 188,332 officers and men with 732 heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>. At 8:00 a.m. on 18 August Wilhelm, whose chief of staff was Moltke, ordered an advance. General Manstein, with artillery of the Hessian 25th Infantry Division, advanced on Amanvillers. The Germans met murderous fire from the French Chassepot rifle and the mitrailleuse before their shorter-ranged needle-guns could reply. Steinmetz's VII and VIII Corps attacked repeatedly across the Mance Ravine and failed with enormous casualties; by 18:30 a large part of them panicked and withdrew.</p><p>At 16:50 the Prussian Guards of the Second Army attacked St. Privat, held by Marshal Canrobert, and were pinned down on the slopes by French fire. After a mass artillery barrage, the Guards, the II Corps and the Saxon XII Corps attacked again and took St. Privat by 20:00, forcing the decimated French back. General Bourbaki refused to commit the Old Guard, having judged the situation a defeat, with ammunition spent and a quarter of his men lost.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> retreated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> on the morning of 19 August, and its last chance of retreating west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> was gone. After a siege of over two months it surrendered on 27 October 1870. The Prussian and Hessian force lost 20,163 killed, wounded or missing, including about 8,000 of 18,000 men of the Guards Corps; the French lost 12,275, though the record is incomplete. Most Prussians fell to the Chassepot, most French to the shells of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krupp">Krupp</a>'s steel breech-loading guns. It was the largest battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Gravelotte” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By a military convention with Prussia in 1867 the Hessian troops were brought into the Prussian system, and in the Franco-Prussian War the Hessian division fought in the <b>Battle of Gravelotte–St. Privat</b> in August 1870.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Military</a></li></ul></section>
